var MED_SCENARIOS = [{"dispatch":"Engine 13, chest pain, 58-year-old male, 1420 W Palm Ln.","narrative":"You find him on the couch, clutching his chest. 'Pressure \u2014 like an elephant sitting on me.' Started 20 minutes ago while mowing the lawn. Pain radiates to his left arm and jaw. He's sweating through his shirt and looks gray.","vitals":{"bp":"148/92","hr":"108","rr":"22","spo2":"94%","temp":"98.2","glucose":"118","pain":"8/10 pressure","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale, diaphoretic","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Acute MI","Anxiety attack","GERD","Muscle strain"],"answer":0,"why":"Pressure-like chest pain radiating to arm and jaw, sudden onset with exertion, diaphoresis, nausea risk \u2014 textbook MI. Tachycardia and borderline SpO2 show the heart struggling. Nothing here says anxiety: no hyperventilation pattern, no tingling, and he's 58 with exertional onset.","miss":"You call it anxiety and he arrests in the back of the engine. Every minute of delay is heart muscle dying \u2014 'time is muscle' isn't a slogan.","actions":"Sit him up. Oxygen if hypoxic per protocol. Aspirin per protocol if no allergy. Monitor, ALS, rapid transport. Calm, fast, no wasted motion."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, chest pain, 24-year-old female, apartment complex.","narrative":"She's crying, breathing fast, says her heart is racing and her fingers are tingling. Big fight with her boyfriend 10 minutes ago. Pain is sharp, 'comes and goes,' points with one finger to the left side of her chest.","vitals":{"bp":"138/84","hr":"122","rr":"28","spo2":"99%","temp":"98.6","glucose":"104","pain":"4/10 sharp","mental":"Alert, tearful","skin":"Flushed, dry","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Acute MI","Anxiety / panic attack","Pulmonary embolism","Asthma attack"],"answer":1,"why":"Young, clear emotional trigger, hyperventilation with tingling fingers (blowing off CO2), sharp fleeting pain, SpO2 99% \u2014 panic attack. But note the rule: you still work it up the same. The vitals that reassure are the normal SpO2 and the hyperventilation pattern, not the fast pulse.","miss":"You dismiss it outright and skip the workup \u2014 and the one time it's actually cardiac in a young patient, you're the one who 'knew' it was anxiety.","actions":"Full assessment anyway \u2014 vitals, OPQRST, history. Coach slow breathing. Monitor. Transport for evaluation; let the hospital rule out cardiac."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, allergic reaction, 30-year-old male, park ramada.","narrative":"Bee sting 10 minutes ago. Hives spreading up his arms, lips swelling, voice getting hoarse. He's scratching at his neck and says it feels 'tight.'","vitals":{"bp":"88/54","hr":"128","rr":"30","spo2":"91%","temp":"98.4","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"2/10 throat tightness","mental":"Alert, agitated","skin":"Hives, flushed","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Asthma attack","Anaphylaxis","Panic attack","Angioedema only"],"answer":1,"why":"Exposure plus hives plus airway involvement (hoarse, throat tight) plus hypotension and hypoxia \u2014 that's anaphylaxis, full criteria. The dropping BP and SpO2 mean it's systemic, not just skin. Stridor risk is minutes away.","miss":"You give him Benadryl and wait. The airway closes, the pressure tanks, and now you're doing a surgical airway conversation nobody wants.","actions":"Epi auto-injector assist per protocol, mid-thigh, immediately. Supine, legs up unless breathing's worse sitting. High-flow O2. ALS. This is a load-and-go."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, difficulty breathing, 19-year-old female, dorm room.","narrative":"Known asthmatic, left her inhaler at home. Wheezing you can hear from the doorway, can't finish a sentence, tripod position on the bed. No hives, no swelling, no exposure.","vitals":{"bp":"142/88","hr":"118","rr":"32","spo2":"89%","temp":"98.8","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"0/10","mental":"Alert, focused on breathing","skin":"Pale","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Anaphylaxis","Severe asthma attack","Panic attack","COPD exacerbation"],"answer":1,"why":"Wheezing, known asthma, no trigger exposure, no hives or swelling, no hypotension \u2014 severe asthma. SpO2 89% with inability to speak full sentences means this is severe, not mild. She's 19, so COPD is off the table.","miss":"You treat it like panic and coach breathing while her airways keep closing. Silent chest is next \u2014 and silent chest means no air moving at all.","actions":"Sit upright. Oxygen per protocol. Assist with her inhaler if available per protocol. ALS. Calm coaching \u2014 panic stacks on top of bronchospasm."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, possible stroke, 71-year-old male, residence.","narrative":"Wife says he was fine at breakfast 40 minutes ago, then his speech went slurred and the right side of his face drooped. Right arm drifts down when you hold both up. He looks confused.","vitals":{"bp":"178/102","hr":"88","rr":"18","spo2":"96%","temp":"98.4","glucose":"112","pain":"0/10","mental":"Alert, confused, slurred speech","skin":"Normal","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Stroke","Hypoglycemia","Bell's palsy","Intoxication"],"answer":0,"why":"Face droop plus arm drift plus slurred speech with sudden onset \u2014 BE FAST positive three ways. Glucose 112 rules out the great mimic. Last known well 40 minutes ago means he's inside the treatment window \u2014 that clock is everything.","miss":"You chalk it up to 'he's old and confused' and the window closes. Every minute of untreated stroke kills about 2 million neurons.","actions":"Note last known well time and report it. Glucose already checked. Nothing by mouth. Position of comfort, oxygen per protocol, rapid transport to a stroke center, ALS."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, altered mental status, 68-year-old female, residence.","narrative":"Daughter says mom is 'acting drunk' \u2014 slurred speech, confused, unsteady. No alcohol in the house. She's sweaty and pale. Daughter mentions mom takes insulin.","vitals":{"bp":"142/86","hr":"112","rr":"20","spo2":"97%","temp":"98.1","glucose":"38","pain":"0/10","mental":"Confused, slurred","skin":"Pale, cool, sweaty","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Stroke","Hypoglycemia","Intoxication","TIA"],"answer":1,"why":"Glucose 38 settles it \u2014 severe hypoglycemia wearing a stroke costume. Sweaty, pale, cool skin plus tachycardia is the sympathetic response to low sugar. The insulin history is the flashing sign.","miss":"You run it as a stroke, bypass the glucose check, and transport a patient whose brain is starving for sugar that you could have fixed in minutes.","actions":"Oral glucose per protocol \u2014 she's altered but check if she can swallow and protect her airway; if not, nothing by mouth. ALS, rapid transport. Recheck glucose."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, altered mental status, 22-year-old male, apartment.","narrative":"Roommate says he's type 1 diabetic, been 'out of it' for a day, getting worse. Deep, rapid breathing you can hear across the room. Fruity smell on his breath. Skin dry and warm.","vitals":{"bp":"108/72","hr":"122","rr":"28 deep","spo2":"97%","temp":"99.8","glucose":"512","pain":"0/10","mental":"Lethargic, confused","skin":"Dry, warm, flushed","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Alcohol intoxication","DKA / hyperglycemia","Anxiety","Sepsis"],"answer":1,"why":"Slow onset over a day, fruity breath, Kussmaul breathing, dry warm skin, glucose 512 \u2014 DKA. The opposite of hypoglycemia in every way: slow, dry, deep breathing. The fruity breath is acetone \u2014 the body burning fat because it can't use glucose.","miss":"You assume intoxication and let him sleep it off. DKA progresses to coma and death without insulin and fluids \u2014 this is an ICU patient.","actions":"Oxygen per protocol, monitor, rapid transport, ALS. Nothing by mouth. Don't try to fix the sugar in the field \u2014 recognize, stabilize, transport."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, unresponsive, 27-year-old male, alley behind convenience store.","narrative":"Bystander says he 'nodded off' 20 minutes ago and won't wake up. Needle on the ground nearby. Barely breathing \u2014 slow, shallow. Lips dusky.","vitals":{"bp":"104/68","hr":"52","rr":"6 shallow","spo2":"82%","temp":"97.2","glucose":"94","pain":"Unresponsive","mental":"Unresponsive","skin":"Cyanotic lips","pupils":"Pinpoint"},"options":["Opioid overdose","Stroke","Hypoglycemia","Head injury"],"answer":0,"why":"The triad: pinpoint pupils, depressed respirations, unresponsiveness \u2014 plus paraphernalia and a normal glucose. RR 6 with SpO2 82% means he's dying from not breathing, right now.","miss":"You wait for ALS before ventilating. He doesn't have minutes \u2014 hypoxia is killing brain cells while you stand there.","actions":"Ventilate with the BVM immediately \u2014 oxygen can't wait for the naloxone. Naloxone per protocol. ALS. Expect him to wake up combative."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, heat emergency, 45-year-old male roofer, construction site.","narrative":"Coworkers say he got confused, then collapsed. Skin is hot and dry, flushed red. Barely sweating despite 110-degree heat. Not making sense when he tries to talk.","vitals":{"bp":"148/90","hr":"132","rr":"28","spo2":"95%","temp":"104.8","glucose":"110","pain":"0/10","mental":"Confused, agitated","skin":"Hot, dry, flushed","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Heat stroke","Heat exhaustion","Dehydration","MI"],"answer":0,"why":"Temp 104.8 plus altered mental status \u2014 that's heat stroke, full stop. The brain is the line: confused means organs are failing. Hot and dry in extreme heat with collapse seals it.","miss":"You call it exhaustion, give water, and wait. Heat stroke cooks organs \u2014 every minute of delay is kidney, liver, and brain damage.","actions":"Cool aggressively: ice packs to neck, armpits, groin. Remove from heat. ALS, rapid transport. Don't give fluids to an altered patient."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, heat emergency, 33-year-old female, hiking trail.","narrative":"Hiking in the heat, now weak, nauseated, headache. Sweating heavily. She's annoyed but mentating completely normally \u2014 knows where she is, what day it is.","vitals":{"bp":"118/76","hr":"108","rr":"20","spo2":"98%","temp":"100.9","glucose":"102","pain":"Headache 4/10","mental":"Alert, oriented","skin":"Pale, sweaty","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Heat stroke","Heat exhaustion","Food poisoning","MI"],"answer":1,"why":"Sweaty, weak, nauseated \u2014 but mentation is completely normal and temp is 100.9, not 104+. That's heat exhaustion. The brain is the dividing line, and hers is fine.","miss":"You overtriage to heat stroke and ice-bath someone who needed shade and water \u2014 or worse, you undertriage the next one because 'last time it was nothing.' Read the mentation, not just the thermometer.","actions":"Shade, rest, passive cooling, fluids if alert and can swallow. Monitor \u2014 exhaustion becomes stroke if you ignore it. Transport for evaluation."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, altered mental status, 78-year-old female, assisted living.","narrative":"Staff says she's 'not herself' today \u2014 confused, didn't eat. Feels warm. History of UTIs. Urine smells foul. She's lethargic but arousable.","vitals":{"bp":"92/58","hr":"118","rr":"26","spo2":"93%","temp":"102.8","glucose":"142","pain":"0/10","mental":"Lethargic, confused","skin":"Warm, dry","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Sepsis","Dehydration","UTI only","Flu"],"answer":0,"why":"Fever plus tachycardia plus tachypnea plus hypotension plus altered \u2014 that's systemic infection, likely urosepsis from the UTI. The vitals show the body losing the fight: pressure dropping, heart racing to compensate, breathing fast.","miss":"You write 'UTI, stable' and transport routine. Sepsis kills fast in the elderly \u2014 this patient is circling and needs a sepsis alert, not a slow ride.","actions":"Oxygen per protocol, keep warm, monitor, rapid transport, sepsis alert to the hospital. Note the suspected source (urine) for the handoff."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, MVA, 40-year-old male, intersection.","narrative":"T-boned on the driver side. He's gasping, trachea visibly shifted, neck veins bulging. Breath sounds absent on the left. Getting more altered by the minute.","vitals":{"bp":"88/60","hr":"130","rr":"34","spo2":"86%","temp":"98.2","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"10/10 chest","mental":"Anxious, deteriorating","skin":"Pale, sweaty","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Simple pneumothorax","Tension pneumothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Hemothorax"],"answer":1,"why":"Tracheal deviation plus JVD plus absent breath sounds on one side plus hypotension and hypoxia \u2014 tension pneumothorax. Pressure is building in the chest, crushing venous return. That's why the pressure is tanking.","miss":"You treat it as a simple pneumo and transport. Tension kills by obstructing blood flow back to the heart \u2014 he arrests from empty ventricles, not from the lung.","actions":"High-flow oxygen, rapid transport, ALS \u2014 needle decompression per protocol if trained and authorized. This is the definition of load-and-go."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, chest trauma, 35-year-old male, industrial accident.","narrative":"Crush injury to the chest. Neck veins distended, heart sounds distant and muffled, BP falling. Pulse pressure narrowing \u2014 86/70 and dropping.","vitals":{"bp":"86/70","hr":"124","rr":"28","spo2":"92%","temp":"97.8","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"9/10 chest","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale, cool","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Tension pneumothorax","Cardiac tamponade","Acute MI","Hypovolemia"],"answer":1,"why":"Beck's triad: JVD, muffled heart sounds, hypotension \u2014 plus a narrowing pulse pressure after chest trauma. Blood is filling the pericardial sac and strangling the heart. Unlike tension pneumo, breath sounds are present.","miss":"You chase the hypotension with the wrong fix. Tamponade is obstructive shock \u2014 the heart can't fill. Fluids won't fix a sac full of blood.","actions":"High-flow oxygen, rapid transport to a trauma center, ALS. Minimal scene time. This needs a surgeon, not a workup."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, GSW, 25-year-old male, parking lot.","narrative":"Single GSW to the thigh. Tourniquet already placed by PD, bleeding controlled. He's pale, sweaty, breathing fast, getting quiet.","vitals":{"bp":"94/62","hr":"132","rr":"24","spo2":"96%","temp":"97.4","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"7/10 leg","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale, cool, clammy","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Hemorrhagic shock","Neurogenic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Sepsis"],"answer":0,"why":"GSW plus blood loss plus pale/cool/clammy plus tachycardia plus borderline hypotension \u2014 hemorrhagic shock, compensated for now. The tachycardia is the body compensating; the pressure hasn't crashed yet because the heart is working overtime.","miss":"The tourniquet is on so you relax. Compensated shock becomes decompensated shock fast \u2014 and 'getting quiet' is the mentation starting to go.","actions":"Verify the tourniquet is tight and effective. Keep warm \u2014 hypothermia kills clotting. Oxygen per protocol. Rapid trauma transport, ALS. Reassess constantly."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, diving accident, 22-year-old male, lake.","narrative":"Dove into shallow water, neck pain, can't move his legs. Skin below the chest is warm and dry and flushed \u2014 weirdly pink. Arms weak.","vitals":{"bp":"88/56","hr":"48","rr":"16","spo2":"97%","temp":"98.0","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"Neck 6/10","mental":"Alert","skin":"Warm, dry, flushed below chest","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Hypovolemic shock","Neurogenic shock","Cardiogenic shock","Heat emergency"],"answer":1,"why":"Spinal injury plus warm/dry/flushed skin plus BRADYcardia plus hypotension \u2014 neurogenic shock. The sympathetic system is offline below the injury, so vessels dilate (warm skin) and the heart slows (no sympathetic drive). It's the opposite of hypovolemic shock's cold/clammy/tachycardic picture.","miss":"You treat the numbers like bleeding and miss the spine. Moving him wrong turns a cord injury into a permanent one.","actions":"Full spinal precautions \u2014 do not move him without immobilization. Keep warm anyway. Oxygen, monitor, ALS, trauma center. Handle like glass."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, altered mental status, 45-year-old male, sidewalk.","narrative":"Bystanders saw him shaking on the ground a few minutes ago. Now he's confused, sleepy, doesn't know where he is. Bitten tongue, wet pants. Nobody saw him drink anything.","vitals":{"bp":"138/84","hr":"104","rr":"18","spo2":"96%","temp":"98.6","glucose":"98","pain":"0/10","mental":"Confused, drowsy","skin":"Normal","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Postictal state","Hypoglycemia","Intoxication","Head injury"],"answer":0,"why":"Witnessed seizure plus bitten tongue plus incontinence plus normal glucose \u2014 postictal state. The history is the diagnosis: someone SAW the seizure. Glucose 98 rules out the mimic.","miss":"You assume intoxication because he's confused on a sidewalk, skip the workup, and miss the head injury he got when he fell during the seizure.","actions":"Protect, monitor, vitals, check for injuries from the fall. Transport. The confusion resolves \u2014 your job is to make sure nothing else is hiding underneath."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, seizure, 30-year-old female, residence.","narrative":"Husband says she's been seizing for over 6 minutes straight, hasn't stopped, hasn't woken up. This is not her normal pattern.","vitals":{"bp":"150/95","hr":"128","rr":"24","spo2":"91%","temp":"99.2","glucose":"112","pain":"Unresponsive","mental":"Actively seizing","skin":"Flushed, sweaty","pupils":"\u2014"},"options":["Status epilepticus","Normal seizure","Pseudoseizure","Syncope"],"answer":0,"why":"Seizing over 5 minutes without regaining consciousness \u2014 status epilepticus by definition. The brain is in a loop it can't break, and every minute risks permanent injury.","miss":"You wait for it to stop on its own. It won't. Prolonged seizures cause brain damage and the airway is unprotected the whole time.","actions":"Protect from injury, don't restrain, nothing in the mouth. Time it. Position to protect the airway, oxygen, ALS immediately \u2014 this needs benzodiazepines per protocol and a hospital."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, allergic reaction, 40-year-old female, restaurant.","narrative":"Ate shrimp, now itchy hives on her arms and neck. Breathing fine, no swelling of lips or tongue, voice normal. She's annoyed, not scared.","vitals":{"bp":"128/78","hr":"92","rr":"18","spo2":"98%","temp":"98.6","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"Itching 3/10","mental":"Alert","skin":"Hives, arms/neck","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Anaphylaxis","Allergic reaction \u2014 monitor","Asthma","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":1,"why":"Hives and itching with completely normal airway, breathing, and circulation \u2014 allergic reaction, not anaphylaxis. No exposure-plus-systemic-involvement. But anaphylaxis can evolve, so 'monitor' means actually monitor.","miss":"You blow it off as 'just hives' and don't watch her. Biphasic reactions are real \u2014 she can look fine and then crash 30 minutes later.","actions":"Monitor vitals and airway continuously. Antihistamine per protocol. Transport \u2014 she needs observation even though she's stable now. Warn her what worsening looks like."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, difficulty breathing, 74-year-old male, residence.","narrative":"History of heart failure. Woke up gasping, coughing up pink frothy sputum. Crackles in both lung bases you can hear without the stethoscope. Ankles swollen, neck veins distended.","vitals":{"bp":"172/98","hr":"118","rr":"30","spo2":"88%","temp":"98.2","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"0/10","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale, sweaty","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["CHF / pulmonary edema","Pneumonia","Asthma","COPD exacerbation"],"answer":0,"why":"Pink frothy sputum plus bilateral crackles plus JVD plus ankle edema plus heart failure history \u2014 pulmonary edema. The left heart can't keep up, fluid backs into the lungs. SpO2 88% confirms he's drowning in it.","miss":"You lay him flat for transport 'for comfort.' Supine lets fluid flood the airways \u2014 you've just made the drowning worse.","actions":"Sit upright, legs dangling. High-flow oxygen per protocol. Monitor, rapid transport, ALS. Position is treatment here."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, difficulty breathing, 66-year-old female, residence.","narrative":"Fever for three days, productive cough with green sputum. Crackles in the right lower lobe only. Getting more short of breath today.","vitals":{"bp":"132/84","hr":"108","rr":"26","spo2":"92%","temp":"102.4","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"Chest 3/10 with cough","mental":"Alert","skin":"Flushed","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["CHF","Pneumonia","Pulmonary embolism","Bronchitis"],"answer":1,"why":"Fever plus productive cough plus localized crackles (one lobe, not both bases) \u2014 pneumonia. The infection is in the lung tissue itself. Unlike CHF, there's no JVD, no edema, no pink frothy sputum.","miss":"Fever plus tachypnea plus tachycardia in an older adult can also be early sepsis \u2014 don't anchor so hard on pneumonia that you miss the systemic picture. Either way she needs the hospital, fast.","actions":"Oxygen per protocol, monitor, transport. BSI \u2014 respiratory infections go both ways. Note the fever and vitals trend for the handoff."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, chest pain, 52-year-old female, office.","narrative":"Sudden sharp chest pain, worse when she breathes in. Heart racing, breathing fast. Flew back from a 9-hour flight yesterday. No trauma, no fever.","vitals":{"bp":"128/82","hr":"122","rr":"28","spo2":"90%","temp":"98.8","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"7/10 sharp, pleuritic","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Acute MI","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Anxiety"],"answer":1,"why":"Sudden pleuritic chest pain plus tachycardia plus tachypnea plus hypoxia plus a long flight yesterday \u2014 pulmonary embolism. The clot traveled from the legs to the lungs. Pleuritic (worse with breathing) points away from MI.","miss":"You call it anxiety because she's anxious \u2014 of course she's anxious, she can't breathe. PE kills by blocking pulmonary blood flow, and there's no field fix, only speed.","actions":"Oxygen, monitor, rapid transport, ALS. Keep her calm and still \u2014 don't have her walk. This one deteriorates without warning."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, abdominal pain, 70-year-old male, residence.","narrative":"Sudden tearing pain in his abdomen radiating to his back \u2014 'like something ripped.' Pale, sweaty, lightheaded. You feel a pulsatile mass mid-abdomen.","vitals":{"bp":"88/54","hr":"118","rr":"24","spo2":"95%","temp":"97.6","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"10/10 tearing","mental":"Alert, anxious","skin":"Pale, cool, clammy","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Ruptured AAA","Kidney stone","Appendicitis","Gastroenteritis"],"answer":0,"why":"Tearing pain to the back plus hypotension plus pulsatile mass in a 70-year-old male \u2014 ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm until proven otherwise. The vitals show hemorrhagic shock developing.","miss":"You palpate aggressively to 'assess' the mass and finish the rupture. Or you work it up on scene while he bleeds out internally.","actions":"Hands off the abdomen \u2014 gentle exam only. Keep him still and calm. Rapid transport to a trauma/vascular center, ALS. Every minute is bleeding time."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, female down, 26-year-old, apartment.","narrative":"Found on the bathroom floor, pale, syncopal episode per boyfriend. Lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding. History of missed periods.","vitals":{"bp":"92/60","hr":"128","rr":"22","spo2":"96%","temp":"98.2","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"Abdominal 7/10","mental":"Alert, weak","skin":"Pale, cool","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Ruptured ectopic pregnancy","Miscarriage","Appendicitis","UTI"],"answer":0,"why":"Childbearing age plus syncope plus abdominal pain plus vaginal bleeding plus hypotension and tachycardia \u2014 ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise. She's bleeding internally; the vaginal bleeding is just the visible part.","miss":"You focus on the vaginal bleeding and miss the abdomen. The real bleeding is inside, and syncope plus hypotension means she's already lost a lot.","actions":"Supine, keep warm, oxygen per protocol, rapid transport, ALS. This is a surgical emergency \u2014 the OR is the treatment."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, abdominal pain, 24-year-old male, residence.","narrative":"Pain started around the belly button 12 hours ago, now settled sharp in the right lower belly. Fever, nauseated, doesn't want to move \u2014 walking makes it worse. Hurts when you press and let go.","vitals":{"bp":"126/80","hr":"104","rr":"18","spo2":"98%","temp":"101.2","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"7/10 RLQ","mental":"Alert","skin":"Normal","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Appendicitis","Ectopic pregnancy","Kidney stone","Gastroenteritis"],"answer":0,"why":"Periumbilical pain migrating to the right lower quadrant plus fever plus rebound tenderness \u2014 classic appendicitis march. The migration is the tell: it started central and localized as the peritoneum got involved.","miss":"Rebound and guarding mean the appendix may be leaking. This isn't 'stomach ache' \u2014 it's a surgical abdomen, and rupture turns it into peritonitis.","actions":"Nothing by mouth \u2014 he may need surgery. Position of comfort (often knees up), monitor, transport. Don't let him eat or drink 'to feel better.'"},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, flank pain, 38-year-old male, residence.","narrative":"Sudden severe flank pain radiating to the groin. Can't sit still \u2014 pacing, writhing. Says there's blood in his urine. No fever, no trauma.","vitals":{"bp":"142/88","hr":"110","rr":"20","spo2":"98%","temp":"98.8","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"10/10 flank","mental":"Alert, restless","skin":"Pale, sweaty","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Renal colic / kidney stone","AAA","Appendicitis","Back strain"],"answer":0,"why":"Flank-to-groin radiation plus hematuria plus writhing (can't find a comfortable position) \u2014 kidney stone. Colicky pain that comes in waves, vitals stable apart from pain-driven tachycardia. AAA would have hypotension; appendicitis wouldn't radiate to the groin.","miss":"The pain is so severe you anchor on AAA \u2014 but the vitals are stable, there's no hypotension, and hematuria points to the urinary tract. Still: any doubt in an older patient, treat the worse possibility.","actions":"Position of comfort, monitor, transport. Nothing by mouth in case of intervention. Reassess \u2014 stones can obstruct and infect, which changes the picture."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, multiple sick, family of four, residence.","narrative":"Everyone has headache, nausea, confusion \u2014 dad worst. Furnace running, it's January, house sealed tight. Dad's skin looks cherry-red. Your partner's headache started five minutes after entry.","vitals":{"bp":"132/84","hr":"104","rr":"22","spo2":"98%","temp":"98.6","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"Headache 6/10","mental":"Confused","skin":"Cherry-red (father)","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Food poisoning","Flu","Gas leak \u2014 natural gas"],"answer":0,"why":"Multiple patients with the same neuro symptoms plus a combustion source plus winter plus sealed house \u2014 carbon monoxide. The SpO2 reading 98% is the trap: the probe can't see CO. Your partner getting a headache inside is the scene screaming at you.","miss":"You trust the 98% and work it up as flu. Meanwhile you're breathing it too \u2014 providers have died on CO calls they didn't recognize.","actions":"Get everyone \u2014 including your crew \u2014 into fresh air NOW. High-flow oxygen per protocol. ALS. Evacuate the structure and call the gas utility. Scene safety isn't a step here, it's the whole call."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, 'drunk' male down, 50-year-old, alley.","narrative":"Smells strongly of alcohol, slurred, confused. But his left pupil is blown and there's bruising behind his right ear. Empty bottle nearby \u2014 and a concrete step with blood on it.","vitals":{"bp":"168/96","hr":"58","rr":"16","spo2":"96%","temp":"97.8","glucose":"104","pain":"0/10","mental":"Confused","skin":"Normal","pupils":"Unequal \u2014 left blown"},"options":["Head injury","Intoxication only","Stroke","Hypoglycemia"],"answer":0,"why":"Unequal pupils plus Battle's sign (bruising behind the ear) plus a bloody concrete step \u2014 head injury with rising intracranial pressure. The hypertension with bradycardia is Cushing's developing. Glucose is normal, so it's not the sugar. The alcohol is a distractor, maybe even the reason he fell.","miss":"'Just drunk' goes in the report and he herniates in the back of the engine. Alcohol explains the smell \u2014 it doesn't explain a blown pupil.","actions":"C-spine precautions, do not let him walk. Monitor neuro status closely, ALS, trauma center. The alcohol didn't cause the pupil \u2014 the brain swelling did."},{"dispatch":"Engine 13, pediatric seizure, 2-year-old, residence.","narrative":"Parents panicking \u2014 their toddler seized for about a minute, now sleepy but wakes when you talk to him. Burning up. No history of seizures. No trauma.","vitals":{"bp":"\u2014","hr":"140","rr":"28","spo2":"97%","temp":"103.5","glucose":"\u2014","pain":"\u2014","mental":"Sleepy, arousable","skin":"Hot, flushed","pupils":"Equal, reactive"},"options":["Febrile seizure","Epilepsy","Meningitis","Head injury"],"answer":0,"why":"Toddler plus high fever plus brief generalized seizure plus no seizure history \u2014 febrile seizure. The rapid temperature spike triggers it, not the absolute number. Postictal sleepiness is expected and he arouses, which is reassuring.","miss":"You panic the parents more by treating it like epilepsy or meningitis. But don't swing too far \u2014 fever plus seizure plus a sick-looking kid who WON'T wake up is a different call.","actions":"Protect, passive cooling (remove blankets, tepid \u2014 not ice water), monitor, transport. Reassure the parents: febrile seizures look terrifying and are usually benign, but he still gets evaluated."}];
